SHAMSU — the four harness defects in one reported turn
=======================================================
2026-08-22 · branch small-shamsu-new
Source: F:\Work\demo2\asteroid-shamsu, session 20260822-090221-f144,
        qwen3.5:9b-q4_K_M. One turn, pasted by the user in full.


THE TURN
--------
    21m52s
    15 reads of js/PlayerShip.js (one row collapsed to `x8`)
    4 failed tool calls
    0 files changed
    final answer ended: "This answer was cut off. That answer hit my per-reply
                         limit of 15,071 tokens..."
    badge:  ✓ SUCCESS  done in 21m52s

Four separate defects. Fixed one at a time, each with its own tests; committed
together because they are one turn's evidence and one story.


1. THE VERDICT LIED
--------------------
    status="stopped" if result.stopped else "done"

"done" meant "the loop returned without raising" - a claim about the PROCESS.
Every surface above it read it as a claim about the OUTCOME.

`_hit_the_length_limit()` was already checked, and the loop already REWRITES
the answer to say it was cut off. It just never told anyone: `SimpleChatResult`
had no field for it, so the truncation died at the seam.

The renderer's own guard could not have worked either -

    ok = str(data.get("status") or "done") == "done" and not data.get("error")

- because nothing ever put `error` in the turn.end data.

FIXED:
  - `SimpleChatResult.truncated`, set at both out-of-room paths.
  - status is now error > stopped > incomplete > done.
  - `error=result.error` is actually published.
  - a third badge, `▲ INCOMPLETE` on yellow. Green was the harness claiming
    success directly above its own "This answer was cut off."; red would be
    just as wrong, because the work up to the cut is real and usually worth
    continuing from.
  - `_turn_verdict` carries the numbers that already existed and never reached
    the one line the user reads:

        done in 21m52s - no files changed - 4 tool calls failed - answer cut off

    A plain Q&A turn is untouched: `done in 9s`. "No files changed" is not news
    on a turn nobody asked to change a file, so it only appears alongside
    something that went wrong.

Also removed here: a duplicated `return self._stop(...)` block, six lines of
dead code sitting directly under the first one.


2. THE READ CEILING DID NOT EXIST PAST THE SECOND NUDGE
--------------------------------------------------------
`ReadLoopDetector` fires at 5 reads (`nudged`) and 8 (`insisted`). Both flags
are one-shot, and after insisting `streak` resets to 0 with nothing counting.

So past eight reads there was NO ceiling at all. The trace shows exactly one
guard line - `read 5 things without producing anything` - and then fifteen
reads of one file across 21 minutes with nothing said again.

The module's rule was "every signal is a nudge, never a stop". That rule plus
one-shot flags is not a gentler guard than a ceiling; after the second sentence
it is no guard at all.

FIXED: `since_insisting` keeps counting past the firm word, and at 8 more reads
returns `READ_LOOP_EXHAUSTED`. The loop still owns stopping - the signal is
returned like any other and the loop decides to end the turn on it. Producing
anything clears it, so a model that reads a lot and then writes is untouched.


3. A HALLUCINATED TOOL GOT THE LIST IT HAD ALREADY IGNORED
------------------------------------------------------------
    ✗ plan FAILED There is no tool called plan. Available: append_file,
      ask_user, contra...

`closest_tool_names` exists precisely to stop this, and its docstring says so -
but its fallback, when nothing is close, is `" Available: " + all 37 names`.
`plan` has no edit-distance match and no shared word with anything, so it took
the fallback. The correction was the repetition it was written to replace.

FIXED: `INVENTED_CAPABILITIES` for names that ask for a CAPABILITY rather than
misspell a tool (`plan` -> "planning is something you do in your answer; write
it to a file with write_file, or just say it"), and the no-match fallback now
names the door (`select_category`) rather than every room behind it. Only
`plan` was seen live; the rest are what the same model families reach for when
trained against a different harness, and each names a real SHAMSU tool.


4. THE OVERSIZE REFUSAL MADE A PROMISE THE HARNESS THEN BROKE
---------------------------------------------------------------
    write_file carried 10,477 chars for fix-plan.md; the cap is 8,000, refused

The refusal's strategy is good and stays as it was. Its last sentence was not:

    "Nothing you generated is lost; resend it in pieces."

`_shorten_arguments` DROPS content arguments from history, and
`MIN_VERBATIM_MESSAGES` is 4. The model read eight more times before coming
back to the write, by which point its own text was `<omitted from history>`.
So it rebuilt the document into its reply, where the reply cap cut it off.

Two ceilings refused the same deliverable, and the second was only reached
because the first had lied about the first one. `_oversized_content`'s
docstring asserted the same false thing ("the model still holds every character
of it") and has been corrected.

FIXED: `_spill_oversized` writes the refused payload to
`.shamsu/oversized/<turn>-<name>` and the refusal names the path, so
`read_file` 60 lines at a time is a real recovery. Under `.shamsu/`, not in the
tree - a half-written document appearing next to the user's source is worse
than the dead end it fixes. Best-effort: if the spill fails the message says
something else instead, and a spill is never a reason to fail a turn.

The 8,000-char cap itself is NOT changed. It is a deliberate, documented
decision (§6.8) with its own acceptance criteria, and one data point is not
grounds to reopen it.


